 | [`test_provenance_paths.py`](test_provenance_paths.py) | Filesystem identity inside the worker, which is where provenance quietly breaks. A Cell that changes its working directory between opening a file and writing it must still produce the same canonical path, and the last test proves that against a real kernel. |
+| [`test_provenance_shared_objects.py`](test_provenance_shared_objects.py) | Whether a tag on an object is a claim about the read that produced it. A tag lives on identity, and equal immutables are often one object — CPython's `None`/`True`/`False`, small ints, empty and one-character strings, `()`; the JSON decoder's `NaN`/`Infinity`/`-Infinity`; NumPy's `True_`/`False_`/`nan`; pandas' `NaT`/`NA`. `set_tags` tagged them anyway, so `json.loads` of a tracked `false`, `null`, `1` or `"\n"` — or indexing one missing cell of a tracked CSV — made every later write of that value name the file as its input: verified in a real worker before the fix (`note.txt`, `flag.npy`, `missing.npy`, plus independent non-finite parses saved in a later cell). Each refusal is paired with a read's own value that must keep its tag, and the small-int case asks the live interpreter which values it shares rather than restating CPython's cache bounds. |
 | [`test_public_api_contract.py`](test_public_api_contract.py) | The public import surface, pinned so the backend behind it can keep moving: package version, constructor parameter names, `run_task`'s calling convention, and the Host and server facades. It pins what a caller can see and nothing more. |
 | [`test_public_exception_projector.py`](test_public_exception_projector.py) | One exception carrying a credential, an absolute path and a shell command, raised on every public surface — HTTP dispatcher, WebSocket chunk, async job result, plan job, REPL job, connector call and remote compute — asserting that none of the three reaches the response while a stable `code` and this daemon's own `request_id` do. `public_failure` is an envelope and never rewrote `error`, so an `except Exception` that wrote `str(e)` had the raw text shipped with a tidy code bolted on. The connector call is here twice over: it answered **200** with `{"error": …}`, and the web client only rejects on a non-2xx, so a connector that never ran was reported as one that did. |
 | [`test_r_kernel.py`](test_r_kernel.py) | **Includes the variable inspector's contract, rewritten.** `substitute()` does not substitute bindings from `.GlobalEnv` — the only environment the inspector reads — so every ordinary variable came back as an opaque `symbol` with no type, length or preview, and the previous test wrote that outcome down as the contract. Safety and uselessness had been conflated: reading an ordinary binding runs nothing, only a promise has anything to force. The inspector now probes with `substitute` first and reads for real when the probe says nothing. Stated rather than hidden: on current R a `delayedAssign` binding is forced when inspected, and a promise whose body raises degrades to the opaque answer instead of failing the whole list — which is how this surfaced, one `stop()` turning every variable into "failed closed". Invoking an active binding remains a hard no. Also covers live output, which the R half did not have: the host has always accepted `stdout_chunk` frames and the R worker never emitted one, so a long R cell showed nothing until it finished. Streaming used to happen only between top-level expressions, and this row used to state the limit that followed: a chatty `for` loop arrived in one piece, because mid-expression streaming needs C-level work in a single-threaded interpreter. That stopped being true when the host began draining the cell's fifo — the drain sees bytes as R's connection buffer empties, which happens inside an expression too. The worker-side flush remains, for the expression that ends without filling that buffer. The interrupt path has its own regression pair now: a shell backgrounds a daemon with SIGINT set to SIG_IGN, that disposition survives every exec down to `Rscript`, and R — unlike the Python worker, which installs its handler unconditionally — declines to install its interrupt handler over an inherited ignore, so the R worker was born uninterruptible; asserted as `interrupted=True` under a SIG_IGN spawner against both the protocol fake and real R. And `rm(list = ls(all.names = TRUE))` is a user's idiom, not a kernel kill: the worker kept its loop, helpers, connections and lineage controller in `.GlobalEnv`, so that cell deleted the worker and the session with it; the real-R tests assert the same pid, working file-read tracers and an inspector listing exactly the user's bindings afterwards, and a source tripwire pins the private-environment shape for CI jobs that have no Rscript. |
